            <ArticleTitle>[Acute abdomen secondary to pyonephrosis. Perinephritic abscess with fistula to peritoneal cavity].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>296-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe a case of acute abdomen arising from an underlying urological condition.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS/RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Herein we describe a patient with acute abdomen arising from a pyonephrotic kidney with fistulization to the peritoneal cavity. The clinical manifestations disappeared following nephrectomy by the anterior approach and drainage of the intraperitoneal cavity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although infrequent, it should be taken into account that peritoneal abscess and/or pyonephrosis can cause acute abdomen when they fistulize to the peritoneal cavity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
